   "title": "Lockheed, L-1011 Maintenance Manual, chapter 6 Dimensions and Areas, 6-10-00 Principal Dimensions: Figure 1 Aircraft Dimensions - Front and Side Views (Nov 15/78), Figure 2 Aircraft Dimensions - Plan View (Dec 1/71), Figure 4 General Ground Clearance (Nov 15/75), Figure 6 Door Size and Location Data; EFFECTIVITY: ALL (a scan in the Internet Archive item 'Lockheed L1011 Maintenance Manual', file 'Chapter 6.pdf'), Figure 7 Landing Gear Footprint Dimensions (Feb 15/78)",
   "accessed": "2026-11-24",
   "note": "Lockheed's L-1011 Maintenance Manual, chapter 6 (Dimensions and Areas), as scanned in the Internet Archive item chapter-22_202208: the manufacturer's own dimensioned three-view, used because no L-1011 airport-planning document is openly available. Its figures are measured (tools/trace/l101/); the drawing's artwork isn't copied. It draws the standard-body L-1011-1 (span 155 ft 4 in, 177 ft 2 in nose to the fin's tip, 178 ft 7.5 in to the tail cone, which is the scale). A greyscale scan of about 32 px a meter, its lines 5-8 px thick, so outlines are good to about 0.1 m. The plan is drawn at 0.910 of the side's scale and turned 0.67 degrees (rendered to the side's scale and turned level); the front view is at the side's scale. Against its printed figures: span -0.1%, tailplane -0.6%, height -0.9%, the engine 10.72 m out against WBL 421.90 in (10.716 m), the inlet 0.8% short of the printed 75 ft 11 in, the wing root's leading edge 0.4% short of 70 ft 10 in. The side and front views draw the fuselage 5.92 m tall; the plan and front view draw it 5.83 m wide by line centres against the printed 19 ft 7 in (kept, -2.4%). The nacelle's inlet ring is drawn 2.67 m tall (the printed 8 ft 10 in is 2.692 m, and the side view's nacelle is 2.66 m) but only 2.45 m across, so it's placed round at the drawn height. The centre engine's S-duct intake: its lip 2.39 m tall in the side view and its fairing 2.45 m wide in the plan, against the printed 8 ft 3.25 in (2.521 m); the front view draws its ring about 2.7 m across and isn't used. The fin's tip stands 16.74 m above the side view's ground line (WL 19), at the low end of Figure 4's 55 ft 0 in to 56 ft 0 in (16.76-17.07 m). Windows: the drawing's dots, at the 20 in pitch (25, 27 and 10 between the doors; the 10 behind the wing's root are hidden and carried on that pitch); their size is the L-1011's (the dots are 0.45 m round). Doors: Figure 6's door table (Door Size and Location Data) gives each door's fuselage station and size; the doors are placed there (the side view draws L1-L4 within 0.3 m of them). R1 is 1.18 m aft of L1; the right side also has the forward and centre cargo doors, the lower galley's service door and the bulk cargo door. The flight deck's panes are drawn small and roughly: the windshield and two side panes are placed as drawn. After smooth.py, tools/trace/l101/straighten.py sets the runs that are straight on the aircraft to their least-squares lines (the tube's crown and belly, the plan's side, the fin's leading and trailing edges: the scan zigzags 0.1-0.2 m every half meter) and eases the nose's crown over a meter. Confidence medium. Gear (`gear`): Figure 1 prints the nose gear 29 ft 9 in (9.067 m) behind the nose and the wheelbase 70 ft 0 in (21.336 m); Figure 7 (Landing Gear Footprint Dimensions, Feb 15/78) prints the track 36 ft 0 in (10.973 m), the bogies' tires 4 ft 4 in (1.321 m) apart across and 5 ft 10 in (1.778 m) along, and the nose tires 2 ft 0 in (0.610 m) apart; its outer widths (6 ft 0 in and 2 ft 11 in) give the tires 20 in and 11 in wide. The tire sizes are the FAA's TCDS A23WE (note 21: 36 x 11-16 nose tires) and Goodyear's application chart (50 x 20.0-20 main). The ground is -5.19 m, the mean of the crown less Figure 4's A (8.17 m: -5.30), the fin's tip less O (16.92 m: -5.26), the wing nacelle's bottom less F (1.00 m: -5.11) and the S-duct intake's top less N (11.19 m: -5.07), the middles of its maximum and minimum heights. No flap track fairings: the chapter's views don't draw them legibly."
  }
